# Fixture for the budget-macros UI test `pass_env_file_hash_suffix.rs`.
# Do not remove: that fixture reads this file through the macro-generated
# env_file lookup. `cargo budget-report --derive-limits` appends a provenance
# comment after each value, and the reader strips an inline comment only when a
# SPACE precedes the `#` (`raw.find(" #")`). This file pins both sides of that
# rule, so a change to the strip condition has to be a deliberate one.
#
# The space-prefixed comment is removed before parsing, so the value resolves.
WITH_SPACE_LIMIT=1000000 # derived by cargo budget-report --derive-limits

# Without the space the `#` is part of the value: `1000000#derived` is handed
# to `parse::<u64>()` and the test panics naming the unstripped raw string.
NO_SPACE_LIMIT=1000000#derived-by-cargo-budget-report

# The same failure with an empty comment: the separator is the space, not the
# hash, so even `1000000#` is not a number.
BARE_HASH_LIMIT=1000000#

# Quoting does not rescue a missing space. Comments are stripped before quotes,
# so this line is `"1000000"#note`: it neither starts nor ends with a quote
# pair, and the whole raw value reaches the parse.
QUOTED_NO_SPACE_LIMIT="1000000"#note

# A quoted value WITH the space does work, in that order: the comment goes
# first, then the quotes are stripped, leaving 1000000.
QUOTED_WITH_SPACE_LIMIT="1000000" # derived by cargo budget-report --derive-limits
